# The short list: the changes that still come back to Daniel for the per-head word when a PR would
# merge on a word given on no head, whatever the plan's scope. team-merge refuses such a merge when
# a path the PR changes (each rename's old path too) matches a line that applies to the PR's
# project, naming the path and the line's area. The list is kept short on purpose: what it leaves
# out (test configs, scripts, dependencies, the agents' instruction files, names that merely hold
# an auth word) merges on the approved plan, and a reviewer still names an area by what the code
# does, so a change the list misses, an auth check in a file of any name say, comes back too.
# One pattern per line: its area, optionally followed by @<project>, the registry name of the one
# project the line applies to, or @own, the team's own project (own_project in bin/lib/team.sh);
# a line with no @ applies to every project. Then one space, then an extended regular expression
# (grep -E) matched case-insensitively against the whole repository path, and against it again
# with each camelCase hump split off by an underscore (resetDatabase.py as reset_Database.py).
# The areas are exactly the ledger's guarded areas (guarded_areas in bin/lib/ledger.jq), kept in
# its order: ci (CI and the gate's config), migrations (production migrations and the production
# database's setup), deploy (the hosts' config and the code or config named for the deploy),
# permissions (the owners file, and each project's server-side auth code by name), secrets (env
# and key files, and files named for a secret or credential), and guardrails, the team's own
# guards on its code repository: team-merge with the library and jq it runs, the scripts whose
# events and checks it reads (team-checks, team-heavy, team-next, team-catch-up,
# team-review-status, team-review-check, and bin/lib/review-app, which names the reviewer App),
# team-ask and team-run-approved, team-log, which logs the reviews, words and approvals team-merge
# reads, team-project, the registry's one reader, the launchers (team-worker, claude-coordinator),
# the lease, the hooks, the permission settings, the role prompts, the ledger contract and the
# lifecycle table (lifecycle.json) the ledger holds events to, data-check, the records and code
# lists, the command guard's readers (command_rules.py, command_text.py, outbound.py, kill_rules.py,
# delete_rules.py) and this file.
# team-merge reads this file from its own checkout, so a PR cannot loosen its own check, only the
# ones after it. A project whose auth code moves to a file of another name has it added here by an
# agent-team PR.
ci (.*/)?\.github/.*
ci (.*/)?\.circleci/.*
ci (.*/)?\.gitlab-ci\.ya?ml
ci (.*/)?\.no-mistakes\.ya?ml
migrations (.*/)?migrations?/.*
migrations .*\.sql
migrations (.*/)?((reset|init)_database[^/]*|mongo-init[^/]*)
deploy (.*/)?(vercel\.json|netlify\.toml|fly\.toml|procfile|render\.ya?ml|railway\.(json|toml))
deploy (.*/)?[^/]*deploy[^/]*\.(py|ts|js|mjs|sh|ya?ml|json|toml)
permissions (.*/)?codeowners
permissions@conventioner back-end/(guards\.py|api/(applicant_auth|permissions)\.py|remembered_sign_ins\.py|sign_in_limits\.py)
permissions@conventioner back-end/utils/(application_token|configured_secret|cors|secret_key|security_headers|session_storage|tokens)\.py
secrets (.*/)?\.env(\.[^/]*)?
secrets .*\.(pem|key|p12|pfx)
secrets (.*/)?[^/]*(secret|credential)[^/]*
guardrails@own bin/team-(merge|checks|heavy|review-status|ask|run-approved|worker|lease|log|project)
guardrails@own bin/claude-coordinator
guardrails@own bin/lib/(guarded|ledger-contract\.json|lifecycle\.json|data-check|records|code|command_rules\.py|command_text\.py|outbound\.py|kill_rules\.py|delete_rules\.py)
guardrails@own bin/hooks/.*
guardrails@own settings/[^/]*\.json
guardrails@own coordinator\.settings\.json
guardrails@own bin/lib/(team\.sh|platform\.sh|ledger\.jq|messages\.jq|review-app)
guardrails@own bin/team-(next|catch-up|review-check)
guardrails@own roles/.*
